          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is COPD different from asthma?",
          "answer": "The main difference is how the airflow limit behaves over time.\n\n**Asthma** is a chronic inflammatory condition of the airways. Airflow narrowing varies from day to day and within a day, and it is at least partly reversible. Symptoms such as wheeze, chest tightness and cough are often worse at night or early in the morning and are set off by identifiable triggers. (Source: \"Asthma\", General clinical reference.)\n\n**COPD** is persistent airflow limitation caused by long-term damage to the airways and the lung tissue. The narrowing does not vary the same way, and the damage is not reversible. It usually comes with breathlessness on exertion that gets worse over years, chronic cough, and sputum, in someone with a relevant exposure history. Tobacco smoking is the dominant cause in high-income countries. (Source: \"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)\", General clinical reference.)\n\nThe two can overlap, and the combination is more common in older people with a smoking history. The reference notes that the overlap is treated as its own situation. (Source: both condition documents above.)\n\nThe retrieved material did not cover how either condition is diagnosed in detail, such as the breathing tests used, or how treatment is chosen. The COPD diagnosis needs spirometry, a breathing test, rather than symptoms alone, but I did not retrieve the passage that explains how it is done. For a personal question about your own symptoms, please talk with a doctor or other clinician.\n\nIf you have severe trouble breathing, such as being unable to speak in full sentences, blue lips, or confusion, call your local emergency number now. Do not wait to discuss it with anyone first.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
